 # Gradient Computation via Finite Differences
 
-Also called: Central Difference Gradient, Central Difference Gradient Computation, Central Difference Gradient Estimation, Central-Difference Gradient Operator, FD Gradient, Finite Difference Gradient, Finite Difference Gradient Estimation, Gradient Estimation
+Also called: Finite Difference Gradient, FD Gradient, Central Difference Gradient, Central-Difference Gradient Operator
 
 ## Overview
 
-Gradient Computation via Finite Differences estimates the spatial gradient vector of a scalar volume field at sample positions or voxel centers to provide visual cues for shading, such as directional light or Phong shading [S1]. It solves the problem of determining surface normals when analytical derivatives are unavailable by approximating the gradient using the difference of data values of neighboring voxels [S11]. Unlike the [Sobel Operator](1416-sobel-operator.md), which uses separable smoothing weights to reduce noise, this method uses only 6 neighboring voxels, resulting in less smooth gradients but higher performance [S2]. It differs from [Gradient-based Diffuse Lighting with Alpha Blending](1257-gradient-based-diffuse-lighting-with-alpha-blending.md) by being a fundamental gradient computation primitive rather than a lighting model, and from [Gradient Estimation from Samples](2180-gradient-estimation-from-samples.md) by computing gradients explicitly on the grid data rather than implicitly during ray-casting texture sampling [S2].
