 ## Operating characteristics
 
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel requires a constant number of operations (6 reads, 6 subtractions, 1 normalization) [S4].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the gradient field if precomputed [S4]. If computed on-the-fly during ray casting, no additional storage is needed, but it increases per-sample computation cost.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the gradient field if precomputed [S4].
 *   **Performance:** The central difference operator is faster than Sobel or Zucker-Hummel operators because it requires only 6 sampling points instead of 27 or 54 [S2]. However, it provides lower quality gradients [S2].
 *   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally efficient, and provides sufficient visual cues for Phong shading and directional lighting [S1].
